Note: The segment "Paragraph 1" produces new knowledge by explaining the techniques for binary grouping of elements based on their frequencies, which is a novel contribution to the field.

The segments "Paragraph 2" and "Paragraph 3" produce new knowledge by presenting a generalization to multiple arbitrary groups and proposing an efficient algorithm for finding wgvalues when d>1, respectively. These contributions extend the scope of the previous work and introduce new methods and results that did not exist before this paper.

The segment "Paragraph 3" also produces new knowledge by transforming the problem into binary grouping and solving Equation 5 to compute the value of wg, which is a novel approach to non-binary grouping.
